---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import ProjectFooter from '../../components/ProjectFooter.astro';
const description = 'Core card gameplay and configuration-driven storytelling for Strange Tales of the Ink Pool, made in 48 hours at the 2025 CiGA Game Jam.';
const flow = [
  ['01', 'Observe the pond', 'Discover a koi in the pond and gain new ways to continue the journey.'],
  ['02', 'Enter the dream', 'Summon the koi in a dream and use cards to extinguish the flames.'],
  ['03', 'Transform', 'The koi takes human form to save the scholar, and the story turns toward waking.'],
];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head><BaseHead title="Strange Tales of the Ink Pool" description={description} /></head>
<body>
<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
<ProjectHeader />
<main id="main">
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <div class="kicker"><span>GAME DEVELOPMENT / 2025</span><span>CiGA GAME JAM · 48 HOURS</span></div>
    <h1>Strange Tales<br />of the <em>Ink Pool.</em></h1>
    <div class="hero-copy"><p>Inspired by Chinese tales of the supernatural, a scholar’s encounter with a koi unfolds through shadow-puppet and pop-up-book imagery. I implemented the core card gameplay, bringing the jam theme <strong>“Everything is alive”</strong> into playable encounters.</p><a class="button" href="#gameplay">Watch gameplay <span>↓</span></a></div>
    <figure class="cover"><img src="/projects/ink-pool/cover.webp" alt="Ink-wash mountains frame the game’s Chinese title, with red koi and blue flowing ribbons" width="1920" height="1080" fetchpriority="high" /><figcaption>STRANGE TALES OF THE INK POOL / 2025 CiGA GAME JAM</figcaption></figure>
    <dl class="metadata"><div><dt>ROLE</dt><dd>Gameplay Programmer</dd></div><div><dt>TEAM</dt><dd>8 people · 2 programmers</dd></div><div><dt>DURATION</dt><dd>48 hours</dd></div><div><dt>TOOLS</dt><dd>Unity · C# · CSV</dd></div></dl>
  </section>
  <nav class="chapters" aria-label="Project sections"><div class="wrap"><a href="#contribution">00 / Contribution</a><a href="#gameplay">01 / Gameplay</a><a href="#configuration">02 / Configuration</a><a href="#debugging">03 / Debugging</a></div></nav>
  <section id="contribution" class="section wrap overview">
    <header><p class="eyebrow">00 / MY CONTRIBUTION</p><h2>A story played<br />one card at a time.</h2><p>Observe, move, light a candle, or summon a koi. Cards act as the player’s verbs, revealing the next encounter as the story unfolds.</p></header>
    <div class="contribution"><h3>What I built</h3><ul><li>Core card rules, hand layout, selection feedback, card rewards, and card consumption.</li><li>Target matching by scene-object tags, connecting card effects to interactions and story progression.</li><li>Card and scene configuration integration, using an existing CSV tool.</li></ul><p>I worked with the designer and the other programmer to define the configuration fields. The other programmer primarily handled Spine animation work.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section id="gameplay" class="section wrap">
    <header class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">01 / THE PLAYABLE LOOP</p><h2>Actions become the next chapter.</h2><p>Using a card changes the scene and can add new cards to the hand. A small set of effect types carries the player through seven configured scenes.</p></header>
    <figure><video controls playsinline preload="none" poster="/projects/ink-pool/courtyard.webp" aria-label="Full gameplay walkthrough of Strange Tales of the Ink Pool"><source src="/projects/ink-pool/gameplay.mp4" type="video/mp4" /></video><figcaption>FULL PLAYTHROUGH / APPROX. 65 SECONDS · ORIGINAL GAME AUDIO</figcaption></figure>
    <p class="submission"><a href="https://www.gmhub.com/game/7508" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original Jam Submission (Chinese) ↗</a></p>
    <ol class="flow">{flow.map(([number, title, text]) => <li><span class="eyebrow">{number}</span><h3>{title}</h3><p>{text}</p></li>)}</ol>
    <div class="feature"><figure><img src="/projects/ink-pool/card-selected.webp" alt="The selected Enter the Dream card is raised above the neighboring Fire Starter card in the hand" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>HAND INTERACTION / THE SELECTED CARD RISES ABOVE THE HAND</figcaption></figure><div><p class="eyebrow">FROM INPUT TO FEEDBACK</p><h3>Select. Confirm. Rearrange.</h3><p>Selecting a card raises it above the hand; clicking it again uses it. As cards are awarded or consumed, the remaining cards move into their new positions.</p></div></div>
  </section>
  <section id="configuration" class="tinted"><div class="section wrap">
    <header class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / WORKING WITH THE DESIGNER</p><h2>From a card idea<br />to a playable rule.</h2><p>The designer proposed cards and their intended behaviors. Together with the other programmer, we discussed which values those behaviors needed and turned them into configuration fields. The designer then authored the content in spreadsheets.</p></header>
    <div class="runtime"><h3>From configuration to playable cards</h3>
      <p>Card definitions are loaded from CSV and indexed by ID. Initial hands and rewards use these IDs to create playable cards, each referencing its definition for artwork and effects.</p>
      <figure class="card-diagram"><div class="diagram-scroll"><img src="/projects/ink-pool/card-lifecycle.svg" width="1100" height="520" alt="CSV tables supply card definitions indexed by ID. Initial hand or reward IDs call AddCard(id) to create a card instance and UI that reference the definition. Selecting and confirming resolves the effect, awards new cards, and removes only the instance and UI if consumed." loading="lazy" /></div><figcaption>Consuming a card removes its runtime instance and UI; the definition remains available for reuse. <a href="/projects/ink-pool/card-lifecycle.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open diagram ↗</a></figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="example"><div><p class="eyebrow">A CARD DEFINED IN DATA</p><h3>“Observe the pond”</h3><p>Observing the pond and extinguishing fires use the same interaction logic. Different configurations select the target, animation, and rewards, allowing each encounter to advance the story without a separate execution path.</p><p>The target’s <code>phase</code> tracks its remaining interaction stages. Each interaction reduces it by the configured value; rewards are granted when it reaches zero or below.</p></div><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Selected fields from Card.csv · card 201</caption><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Effect type</th><td>Interact</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Target tag</th><td>chitang</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Animation</th><td>jump</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Interaction value</th><td>1 <span class="value-note">— reduces the target’s phase by 1</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Reward cards</th><td>202 | 203</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
  </div></section>
  <section id="debugging" class="section wrap debugging">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">03 / A DEBUGGING CASE</p><h2>Following the hitch<br />to its source.</h2><p>After the scene loaded, the game briefly stalled. I initially suspected redundant card data, but further debugging traced the hitch to loading a large BGM resource.</p><p>I resolved the issue through Unity’s audio import and loading settings. The experience taught me to revisit my initial assumptions and trace a problem beyond the gameplay system I was working on.</p></div>
    <figure><img src="/projects/ink-pool/temple.webp" alt="The scholar inside a blue-lit temple, with a Fire Starter card in hand" width="1280" height="672" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>THE TEMPLE / CARDS CONNECT THE SCENE TO THE STORY</figcaption></figure>
  </section>
  <section class="learning wrap" aria-labelledby="learning-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">WORKING WITH THE TEAM</p><h2 id="learning-title">What I learned</h2>
    <p>Working with a professional game designer gave me a practical understanding of how programmers and designers turn gameplay ideas into implementable rules. Agreeing on what the data needed to express was a shared design task, shaping both the content and the code. The 48-hour jam taught me how closely gameplay implementation depends on that shared understanding.</p>
  </section>
</main>
<ProjectFooter />
</body>
</html>
